<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berenice Troglodytica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Berenike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berenice Troglodytica, or Berenike, was a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> founded by Ptolemy II in the third century BCE. Under Roman rule it was one of the main harbours for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, linked by desert roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptos">Coptos</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Excavations there have found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, Indian pottery, textiles and gems.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica.jpg" alt="Berenice Troglodytica" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Government, damit frei verfuegbar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baranis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Berenice lies on the western shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, about 825 km south of Suez, 260 km east of Aswan and 140 km south of Marsa Alam. Ptolemy II Philadelphus founded it in 275 BCE and named it after his mother, Berenice I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A high mountain range separates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> from the coast, and the city stood on a narrow rim of shore at the head of a south-facing bay, the Sinus Immundus. The harbour is marginal but was improved by engineering. The name Troglodytica refers to the native people of the region, the Troglodytai or cave dwellers, though older Ptolemaic inscriptions read Trogodytai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Berenice prospered through the patronage of the Ptolemaic kings, a safe anchorage and its position at the eastern end of the main road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. The road ran to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptos">Coptos</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, 258 Roman miles or eleven days' journey, with watering stations along it. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myos-hormos">Myos Hormos</a>, Berenice was one of the two main shipping centres for trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Aethiopia on one side and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Tamilakkam and Tamraparni on the other. Nearby mines and the island of Ophiodes, now Zabargad Island, supplied gemstones then called topaz and emerald.</p><p>From the 1st century BCE to the 2nd century CE it was a critical way-station for trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> describes the trade along this coast. The Via Hadriana joined it to Antinoöpolis in 137 CE. In the 4th century it was again an active port, forming a district under its own prefect. After the 6th century the port was abandoned, and the bay has since nearly filled with sediment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Giovanni Battista Belzoni identified the ruins in 1818, measuring the city at 1,600 feet from north to south and 2,000 feet from east to west, and estimating its ancient population at 10,000. The chief ruin is a temple in the Egyptian style whose sculptures preserve the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. Excavations began in 1994 under Steven E. Sidebotham of the University of Delaware, and teams from Delaware and the Polish Centre of Mediterranean Archaeology of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> resumed work in the winter of 2007–2008.</p><p>The finds show strong links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: 7.55 kg of black peppercorns, Indian pottery, sail cloth and basketry, teak, coconuts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and a Tamil Brahmi graffito mentioning Korra, a South Indian chieftain. A fortress about 2,300 years old was found in 2019, and remains of monkeys, cats and dogs from about 2,000 years ago were found in 2020–2021 in what has been called the oldest pet cemetery in the world. In March 2022 excavators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> temple uncovered a marble statue of a Buddha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berenice Troglodytica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Across the Indian Ocean the Ptolemies had built the Red Sea ports of Myos Hormos and <b>Berenike</b> for voyages to the Indus delta, Kathiawar and Muziris; Eudoxus of Cyzicus sailed to India about 130 BCE and returned with perfumes and gemstones, and by the time Indo-Greek rule was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandria, with perhaps half a million people, was the second city of the empire, a port linking the Mediterranean to the Red Sea trade with Arabia and India through harbours such as Myos Hormos and <b>Berenike</b>, where pepper, spices, textiles and gems arrived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
